hey this is presh talwalkar  in a previous video i explained a trick  to solve the following problem  imagine you have x to the power of x to  the power of x to the power of x  and so on repeated infinitely many times  and you want all of that to be equal to  two  how can you solve this problem  now by solve i meant how would you find  an answer in a high school math  competition as fast as possible  i was asked this problem in a high  school math competition and the first  person to get the answer got the points  nearly everyone loved this one minute  video which never claimed to be a  rigorous proof presented in a graduate  level real analysis course  so what was the trick  you could look at this equation and  there's a pattern  consider all of the exponents above the  base  you have x to the power of x to the  power of x and so on repeated infinitely  many times  now all of that is equal to 2.  therefore we can substitute 2  for the exponent of this base of x  so we then have x to the power of 2 is  equal to 2.  now we're looking for a positive root so  we get x is equal to the square root of  2.  and that is correct that square root of  2 to the power of square root of 2 to  the power square root of 2 repeated  infinitely many times is equal to 2.  now some of the people who watch this  video actually had some interesting  follow-up emails to me including andrew  george p  divide dolev oliver and vinit  they tried applying the method i  presented  to another problem  so imagine you have this infinite tower  of exponents  set equal to four  well using the same trick as before  we would then have 4 being equal to x  raised to all these infinite powers  so we end up with x to the power of 4 is  equal to 4.  now if we take the fourth root of four  and we're looking for a positive root we  end up with x is equal to square root of  two  so we seemingly have that square root of  two raised to the power of square root  of two raised to the power squared two  infinitely many times is also equal to  four  and this raises a potential paradox  so we've shown that four is equal to two  but this is clearly absurd  so where is the mistake in the supposed  proof  can you figure it out  give it a try and when you're ready keep  watching the video for an explanation  [Music]  so here's a short explanation  this equation or x raised to the power  of x infinitely many times is equal to 4  this is the mistake this equation  actually has no solution  so any subsequent steps are not true  it's like starting out with the equation  x plus four is equal to x plus two  you could subtract x from both sides and  you would seemingly prove that four is  equal to two but we have not really  proved anything our original equation  has no solution  and similarly this original equation  where we set this infinite tower of  exponentials equal to 4 this has no  solution so anything that we deduce from  that will not be true  so for a little bit of a longer  explanation imagine we have this  infinite tower of exponents  x to the power of x all the way  infinitely many times equal to y  this is known as tetration or it's also  sometimes referred to as power tower  super exponentiation iterated  exponentials or hyperpower  so in order to solve for x we must first  check that a solution exists  in other words you want to check for  values of x that converge to a value  for proof i refer you to the  award-winning paper by arthur kenoble  exponentials reiterated in the american  mathematical monthly in 1981  i provided a link in the video  description  so we can plot out this function  it actually has the following curve  the minimum value is x is equal to 1  divided by e to the power of e  and that has a value of y equals 1 over  e  now when x is equal to 1 y is also equal  to 1  and then the maximum value for which  this converges  is x is equal to e to the power  1 divided by e  and this gives the maximum value of y  which is e  so the range of this function goes  between  1 over e and e  and the domain is between 1 over e to  the power of e  and e to the power of 1 over e  so if you have values outside of these  ranges  you're not going to get a solution  so to bring it back  for y equals 2 this is within the range  of this function so we can find a  solution x is equal to square root of 2.  but for y equals 4 there is no solution  as this value exceeds the maximum value  of the infinite power tower  so this problem reminds us of a very  important point  whenever you have an infinite number of  items you generally need to check for  convergence before you simply start  making substitutions and doing usual  arithmetic to solve for an answer  thanks for watching this video please  subscribe to my channel i make videos on  math you can catch me on my blog mind  your decisions if you like this video  you can check out my books which are  listed in the video description and you  can support me on patreon if you have a  math topic you can email me presh  mindyourdecisions.com

